module Core

import Native

{-
   The host's own answer, which is what a JS build decides a float's digits with.

   **A ruling rather than a shortcut**, and the argument is that `Number::toString` is *specified* to
   produce the shortest decimal that reads back as the number, with a tie broken to the even digit -
   which is the same sentence Ryu's correctness theorem is, clause for clause. Reading a float's bits
   at all on this target would mean the 64-bit `Double`/`I64` bitcast the language deliberately does
   not have, so the alternative is not a slower Ryu but no Ryu.

   **`toString` and not `toExponential`**, which matters for one reason: `toExponential` is shortest
   as well, but it breaks a tie the *other* way - the specification says to take the larger where two
   decimals are equally close, and Ryu takes the even one. A JS build asking for that form has to
   recognize the disagreement and undo it, which costs about ninety lines: a probe one digit past
   the shortest form looking for a five, and then a hundred-and-one-digit expansion to find out
   whether it actually stopped there or the five was itself a rounding. Asking for the form whose
   rule already matches is what makes `Show.yana`'s two targets agree by construction rather than by
   correction.

   That is also the answer to "an algorithm configurable to produce the same result as JS": the
   configuration is which of the host's two specified shortest forms you ask for, and one of them is
   already Ryu's. Nothing native has a rounding policy to set, and adding one would be a knob with no
   caller - see Analysis-Library.md §2.1.

   A `.js.yana` file, so a native build never reads it - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5. It answers the
   same two names `Ryu.native.yana` does, and `Ryu.yana` holds the `Shortest` both produce.
-}
fn doubleShortest(value: Double) -> Shortest = shortestOfText(hostToString(value))

{-
   And the same question at single precision, which the host cannot be asked directly.

   A `Float` is a `number` here as well, so `String` on one answers the shortest decimal for the
   *double* that holds it - the shortest for the wrong type, since `0.1` at single precision is a
   different number from `0.1` and has different digits. What finds the right one is the round trip:
   the fewest digits whose value, narrowed back to `Float`, is the value that was asked about.

   Nine is where the walk stops because nine digits round-trip every `Float` there is, so the last
   step needs no test.

   **This one keeps the tie correction, and that is not an oversight.** `toExponentialAt` is the only
   way to ask the host for a decimal of a stated width, and its tie rule is the one that disagrees -
   so the two functions below still have a caller. What removed them from the double's path was a
   host call with the right rule, and there is no such call at this width. The cost is bounded: the
   probe is one call and rejects almost everything, and `terminatesAt` is only reached by a value
   that already looks like a tie.
-}
fn floatShortest(value: Float) -> Shortest:
    let wide = value :: Double
    let &places = 0 :: Int

    while places < 8:
        let text = hostToExponentialAt(wide, places)
        if (truncate(hostToNumber(text)) :: Float) == value then return evenAtTie(wide, shortestOfText(text))
        places = places + 1

    return evenAtTie(wide, shortestOfText(hostToExponentialAt(wide, 8)))

{-
   Whether the value's decimal expansion stops after `count` significant digits.

   Every finite binary float has a *terminating* decimal expansion, so this is a real question with a
   yes or no answer rather than an approximation - and a hundred and one digits is where the host is
   asked for it, which is the widest `toExponential` takes. That width is exact for every value this
   is asked about: a tie needs the expansion to stop within ten digits at this width, and where it
   does the remaining ninety-one are the value's own zeros rather than a rounding that produced some.

   Read off the text rather than through `shortestOfText`, because a hundred and one digits is four
   times what a `U64` holds and the question is only about which of them are zero.
-}
fn terminatesAt(value: Double, count: Int) -> Bool:
    let text = hostToExponentialAt(value, 100)
    let units = length(text)
    let &seen = 0 :: Int
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < units:
        let unit = String.unit(text, i)
        if unit == 101 || unit == 69 then return True

        -- The sign and the point are not digits; everything else in a `toExponential` result is.
        if unit != 46 && unit != 45 && unit != 43:
            seen = seen + 1
            if seen > count && unit != 48 then return False

        i = i + 1

    return True

{-
   The one case `toExponentialAt` and Ryu disagree about, corrected so that the two targets never do.

   Both pick the digits closest to the value. Where two are *equally* close - where the exact value
   sits exactly on the midpoint between them - `toExponential` is specified to take the larger and
   Ryu takes the even one, which is the same rule IEEE rounding itself uses and is the one worth
   keeping: ties-to-larger biases a whole column of a table upwards, and at single precision the two
   rules disagree often enough to show up in a few thousand random values.

   A tie is recognizable exactly, and it needs two things: the expansion one digit past the shortest
   form has to end in a five, and it has to *stop* there. The first is one call and rejects almost
   everything; the second is the expensive one and is only reached by a value that already looks like
   a tie. Both are needed - `2.0515849165496095e186` passes the first and fails the second, because
   what the host wrote at eighteen digits was a rounding that happened to land on a five.

   `digits - 1` cannot lose a digit or gain a trailing zero. It is only reached for an odd number, so
   it is never a power of ten; and a shortest form whose last digit is zero would have been one digit
   shorter, which is what `decimalLength` reading the *value* rather than a written width means.
-}
fn evenAtTie(value: Double, shortest: Shortest) -> Shortest:
    if shortest.digits % 2 == 0 then return shortest

    let count = decimalLength(shortest.digits)
    let probe = shortestOfText(hostToExponentialAt(value, count))

    if probe.exponent != shortest.exponent - 1 then return shortest
    if probe.digits != (shortest.digits - 1) * 10 + 5 then return shortest
    if !terminatesAt(value, count + 1) then return shortest

    return Shortest {digits: shortest.digits - 1, exponent: shortest.exponent}

{-
   A written decimal, read back into the pair the renderer wants.

   Two forms reach this and it has to take both. `toExponentialAt` writes `d.ddde±x`, which is what
   it always wrote; `String` writes whichever of the plain and the exponent forms the specification
   picks, so `1e-7`, `0.000001`, `100000` and `123456789012345680000` are all inputs here.

   **The trailing zeros are never accumulated, and that is what makes the plain form safe.** A value
   below 1e21 is written out in full there, so `String` can hand this twenty-one digits - four more
   than a `U64` holds - and every one past the seventeenth is a zero standing for a power of ten. So
   a run of zeros is *counted* rather than multiplied in, and it is only spent when a non-zero digit
   arrives after it. What is left over at the end is the trailing run, and it goes into the exponent
   - which is what a separate stripping loop would otherwise be for.

   A leading zero is skipped entirely rather than counted: it is a place in the written number and
   never a digit of the answer, which is what makes `0.000001` one digit and an exponent of -6.
-}
fn shortestOfText(text: String) -> Shortest:
    let units = length(text)
    let &digits = 0 :: U64
    let &zeros = 0 :: Int
    let &fractional = 0 :: Int
    let &afterPoint = False
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < units:
        let unit = String.unit(text, i)
        if unit == 101 || unit == 69 then break

        if unit == 46:
            afterPoint = True
        else:
            if afterPoint then fractional = fractional + 1

            if unit == 48:
                -- A zero before the first significant digit is a place and nothing else; one after
                -- it is only a digit if something non-zero follows.
                if digits != 0 then zeros = zeros + 1
            else:
                while zeros > 0:
                    digits = digits * 10
                    zeros = zeros - 1

                digits = digits * 10 + wideCount(unit - 48)

        i = i + 1

    let &exponent = 0 :: Int
    let &negative = False
    i = i + 1

    if i < units:
        let unit = String.unit(text, i)
        if unit == 45:
            negative = True
            i = i + 1
        else if unit == 43:
            i = i + 1

    while i < units:
        exponent = exponent * 10 + (String.unit(text, i) - 48)
        i = i + 1

    if negative then exponent = -exponent
    return Shortest {digits: digits, exponent: exponent + zeros - fractional}
